{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 510 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "95443a8fff62052c528234c08e42a1a117d7c04c862163dddcaffca438bf220a" :edb/db-before-t 509 :edb/db-after-t 510 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533822 50 #inst "2026-10-05T03:37:45.920Z" 13194139533822 true] [17592186054517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186254338 13194139533822 true] [17592186055273 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533822 true] [17592186055273 1145 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =1+0 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533822 true] [17592186055387 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533822 true] [17592186055387 1145 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =-2y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" 13194139533822 true] [17592186059770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186254341 13194139533822 true] [17592186069427 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533822 true] [17592186069427 1145 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{b} & = \\\\ \\frac{35{b}^{2}}{2b} & = \\\\ \\frac{35{b}^{\\cancel{2}}}{2\\cancel{b}} & = \\\\ \\frac{35b}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $b$ in the numerator and denominator." 13194139533822 true] [17592186074084 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533822 true] [17592186074084 1145 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(3,5,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(4,3,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(4-3)^{2}+(3-5)^{2}+(1-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533822 true] [17592186083147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186254321 13194139533822 true] [17592186083301 1020 #edb/ref 17592186254328 13194139533822 true] [17592186087695 1020 #edb/ref 17592186254335 13194139533822 true] [17592186192844 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533822 true] [17592186192844 1145 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x+k}{9-x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y+k}{9-y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y+k}{9-y} \\\\ x(9-y) & =y+k \\\\ 9x-xy & =y+k \\\\ 9x-k & =xy+y \\\\ 9x-k & =y(x+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{9x-k}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9x-k}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-7.$" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254321 1017 #edb/ref 17592186254322 13194139533822 true] [17592186254321 1089 "q-112960" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254321 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533822 true] [17592186254321 1100 "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{3}-x-2,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254321 1113 #uuid "6369285e-2dff-5b5c-a6d1-64b6011c7055" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254321 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254323 13194139533822 true] [17592186254322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254324 13194139533822 true] [17592186254322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254325 13194139533822 true] [17592186254322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254326 13194139533822 true] [17592186254322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254327 13194139533822 true] [17592186254322 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533822 true] [17592186254322 1041 "selection" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254322 1104 #edb/ref 17592186254323 13194139533822 true] [17592186254322 1110 #uuid "6be6948b-0bbe-50e4-8e11-a063db8462a3" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254323 1034 "12" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254323 1111 #uuid "b7221697-c245-573c-be7d-652163f6e37f" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254324 1034 "20" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254324 1111 #uuid "8b882184-5e97-5056-9ec0-a1434002a13a" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254325 1034 "10" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254325 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254325 1111 #uuid "10d7958e-1868-5d93-8a9d-23272d7e1433" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254326 1034 "16" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254326 1111 #uuid "fa815d57-dd6d-544f-bee3-ecf188611a16" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254327 1034 "8" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254327 1111 #uuid "5b605b48-1f56-5b52-a0a4-da41b6e648f9" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254328 1017 #edb/ref 17592186254329 13194139533822 true] [17592186254328 1089 "q-288816" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254328 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533822 true] [17592186254328 1100 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-30=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." 13194139533822 true] [17592186254328 1113 #uuid "a3e9b421-3e24-576c-915f-687f04eee71b" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254328 1145 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=6,\\,b=-17,\\,c=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-30)}{6}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254330 13194139533822 true] [17592186254329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254331 13194139533822 true] [17592186254329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254332 13194139533822 true] [17592186254329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254333 13194139533822 true] [17592186254329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254334 13194139533822 true] [17592186254329 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533822 true] [17592186254329 1041 "selection" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254329 1104 #edb/ref 17592186254332 13194139533822 true] [17592186254329 1110 #uuid "f53737ff-b1fd-54c6-926c-e2c069766dff" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254330 1034 "\\frac{5}{6}" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254330 1111 #uuid "3de3be6d-b395-5d09-bbab-10dfdc21ffb0" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254331 1034 "-\\frac{5}{6}" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254331 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254331 1111 #uuid "424f0306-7c2f-54b9-bf17-95e7ad25a8e8" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254332 1034 "-5" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254332 1111 #uuid "f6fde9e0-3b3e-5a8a-8414-2ccd2c3ac7c8" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254333 1034 "\\frac{17}{6}" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254333 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254333 1111 #uuid "57bba0ba-0886-5ff7-9dbe-f633d561ea32" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254334 1034 "5" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254334 1111 #uuid "7faa68bf-704a-519a-a436-11963299936c" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254335 1017 #edb/ref 17592186254336 13194139533822 true] [17592186254335 1089 "q-319506" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254335 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533822 true] [17592186254335 1100 "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-3+3\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254335 1113 #uuid "54147b52-7d93-5805-b5d5-f24fa1587a93" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254335 1145 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/assets/d00bb449e3525bed0543e690ef41895da5ad9bd28632f127aa7d8e5876ce7deb.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254337 13194139533822 true] [17592186254336 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533822 true] [17592186254336 1041 "field-1" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254336 1104 #edb/ref 17592186254337 13194139533822 true] [17592186254336 1110 #uuid "078ceede-3c63-5d57-a57a-728736efe9bc" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254337 1034 "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254337 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254337 1111 #uuid "4b5b2859-3640-5b81-8651-2225339b75bb" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254338 1017 #edb/ref 17592186254339 13194139533822 true] [17592186254338 1089 "q-338404" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254338 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533822 true] [17592186254338 1100 "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254338 1113 #uuid "dc70fedc-2c3c-5334-9413-78a7c2f33957" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254338 1145 "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)=3(1)+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule (continuity) in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+2(1)+5}+2(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+2+5}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254339 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254340 13194139533822 true] [17592186254339 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533822 true] [17592186254339 1041 "field-1" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254339 1104 #edb/ref 17592186254340 13194139533822 true] [17592186254339 1110 #uuid "75721307-a0a5-5c60-97d9-f2a1a5a6ed29" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254340 1034 "2" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254340 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254340 1111 #uuid "0b5cfed7-5fb5-5f34-8a2a-2258069e7061" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254341 1017 #edb/ref 17592186254342 13194139533822 true] [17592186254341 1089 "q-86328" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254341 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533822 true] [17592186254341 1100 "$\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=$" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254341 1113 #uuid "284d9cbf-5f0f-5fe0-8b82-9cf1281e3bcd" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254341 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{-\\frac{1}{2}}=-2.\n$$" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254343 13194139533822 true] [17592186254342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254344 13194139533822 true] [17592186254342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254345 13194139533822 true] [17592186254342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254346 13194139533822 true] [17592186254342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186254347 13194139533822 true] [17592186254342 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533822 true] [17592186254342 1041 "selection" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254342 1104 #edb/ref 17592186254347 13194139533822 true] [17592186254342 1110 #uuid "bc925353-254d-59c1-84b4-1fc5165b70fe" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254343 1034 "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254343 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254343 1111 #uuid "b90d25f3-4baf-58b7-ba76-959ff7f39d33" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254344 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254344 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254344 1111 #uuid "174c89ad-c596-549c-be65-dea6cf4cbf97" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254345 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254345 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254345 1111 #uuid "cd996b13-7796-55ba-890e-b7acf2cf78fe" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254346 1034 "2" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254346 1111 #uuid "b9031b94-926d-5f93-848a-4e524bbb0eb5" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254347 1034 "-2" 13194139533822 true] [17592186254347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533822 true] [17592186254347 1111 #uuid "a7e40331-f8f6-5b35-bb34-2cf41b5efe7b" 13194139533822 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-112960" 17592186254321 "q-112960/selection" 17592186254322 "q-112960/selection/0